{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age following the assassination of his father. His mother Hodierna served as regent until he came of age. He was a key figure in the Crusader states during the 12th century.\n\n**Early Life and Reign**\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His father Raymond II was assassinated in 1152 by the Assassins. Hodierna of Rethel took control of Tripoli as regent for her young son. Raymond III was formally recognized as count in 1155. His early reign was marked by conflicts with the Muslim rulers of Damascus and Homs. In 1164 he was captured by Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim and held prisoner for almost ten years. During his captivity Tripoli was ruled by his mother Hodierna and later by his cousin Aimery of Lusignan.\n\n**Political Role and Influence**\n\n*highlighted section part 1* Raymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. After his release in 1174 he became a key advisor to King Amalric of Jerusalem. He supported Amalric’s policies and later those of his son Baldwin IV. Raymond III was a proponent of peace with the Muslims and often clashed with the more militant faction led by Reynald of Châtillon. He served as regent for the leper king Baldwin IV from 1177 to 1185. During this time he negotiated truces with Saladin and worked to stabilize the kingdom. His diplomacy was crucial in maintaining the fragile balance of power in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 2* In 1185 Raymond III helped secure the succession of Baldwin V a child king under his regency. After Baldwin V’s death in 1186 Raymond initially opposed Guy of Lusignan’s claim to the throne. He briefly allied with Saladin against Guy but eventually reconciled with the king. His shifting alliances reflected the complex and often treacherous political landscape of the Crusader states. Raymond III died in 1187 just before the disastrous Battle of Hattin where Saladin’s forces crushed the Crusader army. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the Crusader states as a whole.\n\n*highlighted section part 3* Raymond III was known for his administrative skills and diplomatic acumen. He strengthened the defenses of Tripoli and maintained its independence despite constant threats. His policies aimed to balance the interests of the Crusader states with the realities of Muslim power. Though his reign ended in failure with the fall of Jerusalem and much of Palestine his contributions to the defense and diplomacy of the Crusader states were significant. Raymond III remains a notable figure in the history of the Crusades and the County of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.3243790864944458}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.39834240078926086}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.675497570540756e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.00840790942311287}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -6.139089964563027e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.768360213347478e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.34407755732536316}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.10625344514846802}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.4570634852570947e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00032181330607272685}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.2361414134502411}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.34933802485466003}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0012173393042758107}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0006393534131348133}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.6689160474925302e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00016330339713022113}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0006909366929903626}, {"token": "2", "logprob"